1. Enforcing Pod Security Standards (PSS)

Kubernetes features a built-in Pod Security Admission controller. 
You apply security profiles—privileged, baseline, or restricted—directly to namespaces using native metadata labels

| Security Level | Meaning                              | Security 
| `privileged`   |  Almost everything is allowed        | 🔴 Lowest 
| `baseline`     | Dangerous configurations are blocked | 🟡 Medium 
| `restricted`   | Strong security rules are enforced   | 🟢 Highest 

pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: Blocks any pod creation that violates the defined profile
pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: generate a user-facing warning upon creation 
pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: logs violation to the audit log without blocking execution




Example usage: 

apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: secure-apps
  labels:
    # Enforce the strict 'restricted' profile on this namespace
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version: v1.31
